re, concurrency, and low-level hardware interactions.\nL17: Prior efforts such as KGym and CrashFixer have highlighted the difficulty of APR in this domain, reporting low success rates or relying on costly and complex pipelines and pricey cloud infrastructure. In this work, we introduce RGym, a lightweight, platform-agnostic APR evaluation framework for the Linux kernel designed to operate on local commodity hardware.\nL18: Built on RGym, we propose a simple yet effective APR pipeline leveraging specialized localization techniques (e.g., call stacks and blamed commits) to overcome the unrealistic usage of oracles in KGym. We test on a filtered and verified dataset of 143 bugs. Our method achieves up to a 43.36% pass rate with GPT-5 Thinking while maintaining a cost of under $0.20 per bug.\nL19: We further conduct an ablation study to analyze contributions from our proposed localization strategy, prompt structure, and model choice, and demonstrate that feedback-based retries can significantly enhance success rates.\nL20: Comments:  | 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025) Workshop: Evaluating the Evolving LLM Lifecycle: Benchmarks, Emergent Abilities, and Scaling\nL21: Subjects:  | Software Engineering (cs.SE)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2511.15757 [cs.SE]\nL23:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2511.15757v1 [cs.SE] for this version)\nL24:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.15757†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Kareem Shehada [cite17†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 19 Nov 2025 09:12:47 UTC (1,810 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Rethinking Kernel Program Repair: Benchmarking and Enhancing LLMs with RGym, by Kareem Shehada and 6 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite12†View PDF L37:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite18†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite19†view license†creativecommons.or